Description: pvr: timestamp queries from firmware job timestamps
 Implement VK_QUERY_TYPE_TIMESTAMP and vkCmdWriteTimestamp2, which were an
 UNREACHABLE stub (pvr_arch_cmd_buffer.c).
 .
 A timestamp is an availability job for the query on the query compute context,
 behind an event barrier that waits for all previous geometry, fragment, compute,
 transfer and query jobs, so resets and copies of query pools recorded before it
 have finished. The job asks the kernel for a PRE timestamp
 (DRM_PVR_SUBMIT_JOB_TIMESTAMP_PRE, previous patch): the firmware writes the time
 in ns of CLOCK_MONOTONIC_RAW into the query's result before the job starts, so
 the value is in memory before the job marks the query available. Results of
 timestamp pools come from a suballocator whose buffers are created with
 PVR_BO_ALLOC_FLAG_FW_TIMESTAMPS; a pool larger than the kernel's limit
 (bo_max_size, 64 KiB, 8192 queries) fails with VK_ERROR_OUT_OF_DEVICE_MEMORY.
 Results are 64-bit; vkGetQueryPoolResults and the copy program read both halves
 (PVR_QUERY_COPY_DATA_RESULT_64BIT).
 .
 Inside an owned graphics sub command with a single view, the render is split at
 the timestamp the same way pvr_occlusion_query_begin() splits at a pool change
 and the mid-fragment barrier splits for stencil: the render so far is kicked
 with barrier_store, the timestamp is queued as its own query job, a barrier
 makes the following graphics jobs wait for that query job, and the render
 continues in a new sub command with barrier_load. The store writes the tile
 contents, colour and depth/stencil, to the SPM scratch buffer and the load
 restores them as the background object, so the pass sees the same attachment
 contents as without the split, and the timestamp lies between the draws
 recorded before and after it. A timestamp recorded before the first draw takes
 the same path, which keeps load operations such as clears in the first part.
 With multiview, and in secondary command buffers that continue a render pass,
 the timestamp is deferred until the sub command ends: the store and load go
 through one SPM scratch buffer while the render job is submitted once per view,
 and secondaries hand their timestamps to the primary. With multiview a
 timestamp fills one query per view (one job each).
 .
 struct pvr_query_info gets availability_write.timestamp, which
 pvr_arch_add_query_program() passes on to the query job when it is not NULL.
 The occlusion availability write in pvr_arch_cmd_buffer_end_sub_cmd(),
 vkCmdResetQueryPool and vkCmdCopyQueryPoolResults zero-initialize the
 struct, so the pointer and the fields they do not set, is_dynamic_render and
 is_suspend among them, are zero.
 .
 vkGetQueryPoolResults reads the availability word with p_atomic_read(), an
 acquire load, before it reads the result. The device writes the result first
 and marks the query available afterwards; on an arm64 CPU a plain load of the
 result could be satisfied before the load of the availability word. This
 applies to every query type, both to the immediate check and the wait loop.
 .
 The limits do not advertise timestamps yet; that is the next patch.
 .
 Only for this downstream stack; not intended for upstream submission.
